<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">United Democratic Front (South Africa)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1983">1983</a>–<a href="/years/1991">1991</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The United Democratic Front (UDF) was a South African coalition of hundreds of civic, church, student, labour and community organisations launched in August 1983 to oppose the tricameral constitution. Non-racial and aligned with the banned ANC, it led rent boycotts, strikes and protests in the townships during the uprisings of the mid-1980s. It was restricted in 1988 and dissolved in 1991 after the ANC was unbanned.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Trade union activity, beginning in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durban">Durban</a> in 1973, and the student upsurge in Soweto in 1976 had built a culture of mass protest among black South Africans, and the economic crisis that reached the country in 1982 brought mass unemployment to the young. The new constitution of 1983 created a Tricameral Parliament that gave Coloured and Indian South Africans nominal representation and still excluded black people.</p><p>The call for a united front of churches, civic associations, trade unions, student organisations and sports bodies came from the Rev. Allan Boesak at a conference on 23 January 1983. Regional committees were launched in Natal, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transvaal">Transvaal</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-province">Cape Province</a> between May and July, and on 20 August 1983, the day the government planned to introduce the new constitution, the Front was launched at the Rocklands community hall in Mitchell's Plain near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-town">Cape Town</a>, before delegates of 575 organisations and a rally of about 10,000 people. Its slogan was UDF Unites, Apartheid Divides.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>From September 1984 the UDF and its affiliates organised rent boycotts, school protests, worker stay-aways and a boycott of the tricameral elections. The state answered with arrests and with treason trials that charged the Front with being a shadow organisation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-national-congress">African National Congress</a> (ANC). In the Delmas Treason Trial four activists were convicted of terrorism in November 1988, a verdict overturned by the Appeal Court in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bloemfontein">Bloemfontein</a> in 1989. In 1986 President P. W. Botha barred the UDF from receiving foreign funds, which made up more than half its budget, and by late 1987 most of its activists were in prison.</p><p>In 1989 the UDF and the Congress of South African Trade Unions drew together in the Mass Democratic Movement, a loose alliance the government found hard to ban. After the ANC and other organisations were unbanned in February 1990, the Front decided in March 1991 to disband and held its last meeting in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannesburg">Johannesburg</a> on 14 August 1991.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Front had a federal structure, and by 1986 some 700 organisations worked under it. A National Executive Committee with three presidents led it, but much of the momentum came from the youngest members. It adopted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freedom-charter">Freedom Charter</a> but was never formally attached to the ANC and took no part in the armed struggle; unlike the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-consciousness-movement">Black Consciousness Movement</a>, it welcomed white members. A Women's Congress, formed on 23 April 1987, elected Albertina Sisulu to its national council.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “United Democratic Front (South Africa)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Within the country, the Soweto uprising of 1976, the Black Consciousness movement of Steve Biko, who died in police custody in 1977, and the <b>United Democratic Front</b>, founded in 1983, created new forms of mass resistance.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-south-africa">Republic of South Africa · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
